Description:
    A seed file for every model your `recourses` routes serve, so every screen
    has rows to show. Each db/seeds/<resources>.rb holds 25 rows: the first
    carries only what a row cannot save without — whatever a presence or
    inclusion validator demands, every required belongs_to, and every column
    the database refuses NULL in with no default to answer for it — the last
    fills every attribute, and the rest mix which optional attributes are
    filled, so a nullable column is seen both ways.

    Values are of each column's own type, drawn at random while generating — so
    the written file never changes between `rails db:seed` runs. A string is a
    random length inside its length validator's bounds and its column's SQL
    limit — whichever gate is tighter wins — and reads as words, each
    one to fifteen characters joined by single spaces, in an alphabet reaching
    past ASCII into accents and emoji, never repeated within its column — a
    column pinned to an exact length gets one solid word, since a code is not
    prose. An email column gets an address, a phone column ten valid digits, a
    column named like an id digits alone, an enum cycles the words it admits, a
    date and a time step back a random distance, and a reference reads a random
    row of the model it points at. A row a validator or the database refuses
    is skipped rather than stopping the run — the loader's count never sees it
    — and editing the file is how a skipped row is won back.

    db/seeds.rb gains the loader that reads the directory, once, so follow-up
    `rails db:seed` runs pick every file up — alphabetically, so reorder by
    hand where one model's rows need another's to exist first — and say what
    they are doing: `Seeding contacts... ⏳` while a file runs, rewritten to
    `Seeded 45 contacts ✅` once it is done. A resource whose model does not
    exist is skipped, and a file that already exists asks before being
    overwritten.

Example:
    bin/rails generate recourse:seed

    This will create:
        db/seeds/<resources>.rb, one per model `recourses` declares
        the loader line in db/seeds.rb, unless it is already there
